WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Your body can make antibodies that fit a

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.640
bacterium it has never met before.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.280
So how does it design them in time?

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.600
In 1957, the Melbourne scientist Frank Macfarlane Burnet

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.600
proposed that it doesn't design anything.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.320
The right cells already exist before the infection,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.400
and the infection picks them out.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.560
That idea, clonal selection, explains how B cells

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:25.760
target a pathogen.

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:29.686
In the last video, phagocytes held the splinter's

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:33.126
bacteria back, and dendritic cells carried pieces of

00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:34.726
them to a lymph node.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.606
Bacteria and their molecules also drain there in

00:00:37.687 --> 00:00:38.726
the lymph.

00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:41.606
Lymph nodes are packed with lymphocytes, the white

00:00:41.687 --> 00:00:44.086
blood cells of the adaptive response.

00:00:44.207 --> 00:00:47.046
This video follows one kind, B cells.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:50.746
B cells recognise antigens.

00:00:50.987 --> 00:00:53.306
An antigen is a molecule that the immune

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:56.666
system can recognise and respond to, usually a

00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:58.906
protein or a sugar chain on the surface

00:00:58.987 --> 00:01:00.026
of a pathogen.

00:01:00.347 --> 00:01:03.146
One bacterium carries many different antigens.

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:06.586
And a receptor doesn't fit the whole antigen,

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.066
just one small part of its surface, which

00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:10.506
has a particular shape.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:15.493
B cells recognise antigens using receptors, and each

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:18.213
receptor is an antibody held in the cell's

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:19.093
membrane.

00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:22.053
An antibody is a Y-shaped protein.

00:01:22.294 --> 00:01:24.453
Its stem is the same in many different

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:25.573
antibodies.

00:01:25.894 --> 00:01:27.573
The tips of its two arms are the

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:30.613
binding sites, and their shape fits one small

00:01:30.694 --> 00:01:32.093
part of an antigen.

00:01:32.454 --> 00:01:35.413
Both tips are identical, so one antibody can

00:01:35.454 --> 00:01:37.573
grip two copies of the same shape.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.440
On any one B cell, all the receptors

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.120
have the same shape.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.080
But different B cells have different shapes.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.000
As each B cell develops in the bone

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.880
marrow, it shuffles pieces of its receptor genes

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.160
at random.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.440
So before any infection, you already have billions

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.240
of B cells, with millions of different receptor

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.200
shapes.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.680
Most will never meet anything they fit.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.700
In the lymph node, the bacterium's antigens meet

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.820
huge numbers of B cells.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.260
Only the few whose receptors fit one of

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.700
those shapes can bind.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.140
That's the selection.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.620
The antigen doesn't tell a B cell what

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.860
to make, it picks out the B cells

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:18.980
that already fit.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.100
Usually, a chosen B cell also needs a

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.900
go-ahead signal from a helper T cell, one

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.220
of the T cells the dendritic cells alerted

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.340
to this pathogen.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.180
The next video shows how.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.120
Once it's switched on, the B cell divides,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.840
and its daughter cells divide again and again

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.240
over several days, making thousands of identical cells.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.960
That's a clone, and every cell in it

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.200
has the same receptor shape.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.080
This is why a first response takes days.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.480
A few rare cells have to multiply into

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.800
thousands before there are enough to matter.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.960
Many cells in the clone become plasma cells.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.600
A plasma cell is packed with the machinery

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.400
for making protein, and it can release thousands

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.760
of antibody molecules every second, each with the

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.920
same binding shape as the receptor that was

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:09.800
selected.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.480
The blood carries them to the infection.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.080
Some cells in the clone become memory B

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.040
cells instead.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.200
They stay in the body long after the

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.680
infection, and a later video shows what they

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.160
do.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:25.446
Antibodies don't kill bacteria by themselves.

00:03:25.647 --> 00:03:29.166
They bind, and binding helps in three ways.

00:03:29.487 --> 00:03:31.806
They can cover the parts a bacterium uses

00:03:31.887 --> 00:03:34.206
to stick to our cells, or the toxins

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:36.486
it releases, so those can't work.

00:03:36.847 --> 00:03:39.406
With two arms, they can link bacteria into

00:03:39.487 --> 00:03:40.246
clumps.

00:03:40.527 --> 00:03:43.966
And they coat bacteria, so phagocytes, which have

00:03:44.047 --> 00:03:47.246
receptors for the antibody's stem, grab and engulf

00:03:47.407 --> 00:03:48.526
them much faster.

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:51.413
That's what specific means.

00:03:51.974 --> 00:03:55.253
Antibodies made against this bacterium bind the shapes

00:03:55.294 --> 00:03:56.133
on its surface.

00:03:56.614 --> 00:04:00.293
A different bacterium, with differently shaped antigens, usually

00:04:00.374 --> 00:04:02.613
isn't bound, so it has to select its

00:04:02.694 --> 00:04:03.933
own B cells.

00:04:04.214 --> 00:04:07.013
Each pathogen gets its own matching clones.

00:04:08.234 --> 00:04:10.073
So here's the whole sequence.

00:04:10.314 --> 00:04:12.713
The antigen reaches a lymph node, and B

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:15.353
cells whose receptors fit it bind it.

00:04:15.594 --> 00:04:17.993
With a helper T cell's signal, they're switched

00:04:18.074 --> 00:04:20.113
on and divide into a clone.

00:04:20.314 --> 00:04:23.513
Plasma cells release antibodies, and the antibodies bind

00:04:23.594 --> 00:04:25.913
the antigen and help remove the pathogen.

00:04:27.134 --> 00:04:30.653
So remember: clonal selection, the name is the

00:04:30.814 --> 00:04:31.533
process.

00:04:32.014 --> 00:04:34.413
The B cell that fits is selected, and

00:04:34.494 --> 00:04:35.853
then it's cloned.

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:38.586
Two exam questions now.

00:04:39.147 --> 00:04:39.626
First.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.586
A mouse was injected with killed cells of

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:43.786
bacterium A.

00:04:44.427 --> 00:04:47.066
Two weeks later, serum, the liquid part of

00:04:47.147 --> 00:04:49.546
its blood, was taken from the mouse.

00:04:49.867 --> 00:04:52.266
The serum was mixed with bacterium A in

00:04:52.427 --> 00:04:55.386
tube one, and with bacterium B in tube

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:55.866
two.

00:04:56.507 --> 00:04:59.386
In tube one, the bacteria clumped together.

00:04:59.627 --> 00:05:02.186
In tube two, they stayed evenly spread.

00:05:02.827 --> 00:05:05.066
Explain the results in both tubes.

00:05:05.627 --> 00:05:06.906
Pause here to answer.

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:20.373
Bacterium A's antigens selected the B cells that

00:05:20.534 --> 00:05:23.413
fit them, and these multiplied into plasma cells

00:05:23.494 --> 00:05:25.093
that released antibodies.

00:05:25.414 --> 00:05:28.933
In tube one, antibodies bound A and, with

00:05:29.014 --> 00:05:31.733
two arms each, linked them into clumps.

00:05:32.054 --> 00:05:34.933
Tube two didn't visibly clump, so there's no

00:05:35.014 --> 00:05:37.213
evidence the antibodies linked B.

00:05:37.574 --> 00:05:40.053
B may lack the matching antigen, but this

00:05:40.134 --> 00:05:41.893
test alone can't show that.

00:05:42.454 --> 00:05:45.413
Saying they killed A is the classic mistake.

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:47.193
Second.

00:05:47.754 --> 00:05:50.713
Antibodies against a new pathogen usually appear in

00:05:50.794 --> 00:05:52.793
the blood only a week or more after

00:05:52.874 --> 00:05:53.833
infection.

00:05:54.154 --> 00:05:56.953
A student says, this is because B cells

00:05:57.034 --> 00:05:59.433
must first design an antibody that fits the

00:05:59.514 --> 00:06:00.553
new pathogen.

00:06:00.874 --> 00:06:02.753
Evaluate the student's claim.

00:06:03.034 --> 00:06:04.313
Pause here to answer.

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:17.373
The delay is real, but the reason is

00:06:17.454 --> 00:06:18.173
wrong.

00:06:18.494 --> 00:06:21.693
B cells with fitting receptors already exist before

00:06:21.774 --> 00:06:25.293
infection, made by random gene shuffling, so nothing

00:06:25.374 --> 00:06:26.653
is designed.

00:06:26.694 --> 00:06:29.853
The delay happens because those cells are rare.

00:06:29.894 --> 00:06:32.493
They must be selected, switched on and divide

00:06:32.574 --> 00:06:36.093
many times before plasma cells release enough antibody

00:06:36.134 --> 00:06:37.453
to detect.

00:06:37.614 --> 00:06:39.533
Thinking the pathogen tells the B cell what

00:06:39.614 --> 00:06:41.693
to make is the classic mistake.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.180
So for any antibody question, say which B

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.900
cells fit the antigen, how they're selected and

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.340
multiplied, what plasma cells release, and what the

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.660
antibodies do when they bind.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.460
Next, the T cells: helpers that give the

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.060
go-ahead, and killers that destroy infected cells.

00:07:02.267 --> 00:07:03.706
Here are the key notes.

00:07:03.947 --> 00:07:05.866
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:05.947 --> 00:07:06.746
to copy them.
